// D1 round 8 / M0 — A CAPTURED `&Struct` USED AS A PLACE BASE IN A CLOSURE.
//
// THE DEFECT (measured at 266edc21, mlir_gen — an OVER-REFUSAL, not a
// soundness hole). `let s: S = …; let r: &S = &s; let f = || -> i64 { return
// r.peek(); };` self-diagnosed «mlir_gen: internal: `return` value lowered to
// no value» and the compile failed, while
//   • the same call OUTSIDE the closure,
//   • the value-capture twin (`let f = || s.peek();`), and
//   • a captured SCALAR ref (`let r: &i64 = &x; || *r`)
// all lowered fine. Narrowed past the method call: the plain field read
// `return r.v;` inside the closure failed too («expr kind 14»), and the
// `let t: i64 = r.v;` spelling tripped the channel TWICE. So the class is
// "captured reference-to-STRUCT used as a PLACE BASE", not "method call".
//
// THE ROOT (mlir_gen_dyn.cpp, closure env binding). The struct-ref `let`
// registers `var_struct_[r] = key(S)` and puts the POINTEE address in
// scope_[r]; the capture is therefore classified pointer-repr and the env
// correctly carries that one-level pointer. The closure BODY then re-derived
// the key from the CAPTURE TYPE — `&S` — instead of the pointee, so
// `struct_types_` missed and every projection on the capture lowered to
// nothing. One peel, at the one site that had not done it.
//
// VALUES, not just compilation: a self-diagnosis channel that is silenced
// rather than fixed still writes an object file, so this fixture READS through
// the captured reference in three shapes (method receiver, field read,
// let-bound field read) and answers a distinct exit code per shape.
package bc_d1r8_m0_closure_captured_ref;

struct S { v: i64 }
impl S {
    fn peek(&self) -> i64 { return self.v; }
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let s: S = S { v: 7i64 };
    let r: &S = &s;

    let f = || -> i64 { return r.peek(); };      // method receiver
    if f() != 7i64 { return 1i32; }

    let g = || -> i64 { return r.v; };           // field read
    if g() != 7i64 { return 2i32; }

    let h = || -> i64 { let t: i64 = r.v; return t; };   // let-bound field read
    if h() != 7i64 { return 3i32; }

    let x: i64 = 5i64;
    let rx: &i64 = &x;
    let k = || -> i64 { return *rx; };           // the control that always worked
    if k() != 5i64 { return 4i32; }

    return 0i32;
}
